Adds env-var override for the persistent Chromium profile path.

## Why

The current hardcoded `BROWSER_DATA_DIR = ~/.amazon-order-history-mcp/browser-data` makes it impossible to run multiple instances of this MCP on the same host. That's a real need for users with multiple Amazon accounts — e.g. a Personal account and a separate Amazon Business account (separate login email), where each needs its own persistent browser session.

## What

One conditional read of `process.env.AMAZON_ORDERS_BROWSER_DATA_DIR`, falling back to the previous default if the env var is unset. **Zero behavior change for existing users.** Backwards-compatible.

```ts
// Before
const BROWSER_DATA_DIR = join(homedir(), ".amazon-order-history-mcp", "browser-data");

// After
const BROWSER_DATA_DIR =
  process.env.AMAZON_ORDERS_BROWSER_DATA_DIR ??
  join(homedir(), ".amazon-order-history-mcp", "browser-data");
```

## Usage

Two instances of the MCP, one per Amazon account, each with its own profile:

```jsonc
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "amazon-orders-personal": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": ["<project>/dist/index.js"],
      "env": { "AMAZON_ORDERS_BROWSER_DATA_DIR": "<profile-root>/personal" }
    },
    "amazon-orders-business": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": ["<project>/dist/index.js"],
      "env": { "AMAZON_ORDERS_BROWSER_DATA_DIR": "<profile-root>/business" }
    }
  }
}
```

## Test plan

- [x] Existing behavior unchanged when env var unset — verified by running the MCP without the env var; profile still lands at the documented default.
- [x] Per-instance isolation works when env var is set to distinct paths — verified end-to-end against amazon.com and a separately-logged-in account.
- [x] No new dependencies.

